X86 x86-userword-lostpublish-main
(* The lost-publish direction on x86, under upstream's protocol -- the row that
   answers "can this bite us on x86?".

   No. The departing owner's three stores leave the store buffer in program
   order, so its sentinel write precedes the handover store; the arriving owner
   cannot claim until the handover store is visible; and its own publish is
   therefore later in the total store order. TSO supplies for free the ordering
   that the release store has to supply on ARM.

   Expected Never, and the fixed counterpart is Never for the same reason, so
   there is nothing for the change to regress here. See the arm64 pair for the
   architecture where it does matter. *)
{ uw=7; tid=3; lce=5; }
 P0           | P1              ;
 MOV [uw],$9  | MOV EDX,$1      ;
 MOV [lce],$0 | XCHG [tid],EDX  ;
 MOV [tid],$0 | MOV [uw],$8     ;
exists (1:EDX=0 /\ uw=9)
